TS control flow doesn't see closure assignment, so after `if (!best) pass(true)` best is still null. Workaround: type annotate and cast, or check via an accessor. Use:
```
const result: {x:number;z:number;score:number}|null = best;
return result ? {x:result.x,z:result.z} : null;
```
Still TS considers best null. Could have `pass` return best:
```
const found = (relaxed) => { let b=null; ...; return b; }
let best = pass(false); if (!best) best = pass(true);
```
But pass loops and needs best local. Refactor: make pass return `{x,z,score}|null`; local variable inside. Then outer assignment recognized. Let's do:
```
const pass = (relaxed) => { let best = null; ...; return best; };
let best = pass(false); if (!best) best = pass(true);
return best ? ... : null;
```
TS infers return type `{...}|null`, and outer `let best` gets that union; assignment recognized across calls. Because pass's return is explicit. Good.

